<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Choe Je-u</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1824–1864</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Choe Je-u was a Korean religious teacher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak">Donghak</a> ('Eastern Learning') movement in 1860. After a religious experience he preached a faith drawing on Confucian, Buddhist, Daoist and folk traditions, offered as a Korean answer to the 'Western Learning' of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. His teaching spread among peasants, and the government had him executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daegu">Daegu</a> in 1864 for heresy, but his followers carried on the movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/choe-je-u.jpg" alt="Choe Je-u" width="320" height="551" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Choe_Je-u.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
